"Eric" <usenet@brainshell.invalid.org> wrote:

> http://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~eva/nethack/iaq.html#survive

On that site, these claims are made:

  "On a system with varying skill levels, though,
  experts are likely to find more bones than they
  leave. Perhaps the easiest way to see this is
  to take an extreme case. If you are so expert
  that you ascend all the time, then on a
  single-user system you'll never leave or find
  bones. On a multi-user system, you'll still
  never leave bones, but there will be plenty
  there for you to find."

That much seems sound, but this:

  "If experts find more bones, newbies must find
  fewer (since the total number of bones files
  remains approximately stable)."

seems bogus, since it is internally self
contradictory. If the numbers of bones files is
approximately stable, then the rate at which
_everyone_ picks them up is approximately stable,
and it doesn't matter whether the one picking them
up is an expert or a novice, even to that person's
rate of picking them up.

It seems to me that the author has confused "number
of bones files existing" with "rate at which bones
files are created". The latter _increases_ when
bones files are consumed, because the removed bones
files leave extra slots where anyone can drop bones
files, increasing the success at bones file
creation.

The expert who picks up bones files but never drops
them, then, just increases the rate at which others
can drop bones files, thus the "approximately
stable" numbers of such bones files.

I'll grant that some trivial difference must exist,
but I don't think a noticable difference would
exist, when one expert (or ten) joins a site with
several thousand novices.

